<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikephoros Phokas the Elder</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 830–<a href="/years/896">896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikephoros Phokas the Elder was a Byzantine general of the late 9th century and the grandfather of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-phokas">Nikephoros II Phokas</a>. Sent to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 885, he recovered much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, laying the basis of the theme of Longobardia. He later commanded against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> under Leo VI.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nikephoros was the son of a native of Cappadocia called Phokas, the founder of the Phokas family, who caught the attention of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> the Macedonian on a campaign in the 870s and was made a tourmarches. The young Nikephoros was taken into the imperial retinue and soon joined the guard corps of the manglabitai. Before 878 he was promoted to protostrator and given a palace near the Church of St. Thecla, and he later became military governor of the theme of Charsianon, where he won successes against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He replaced Stephen Maxentios, who had been defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, as single commander in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, leading troops of several western themes reinforced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, among them a Paulician detachment. Byzantine sources credit him with recovering Taranto, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, Santa Severina, Rhegion, Tropai and Amantia; at Amantia he kept the news of Basil's death and his own recall secret until the Arab garrison had surrendered under a promise of safe passage. He settled many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> in the region, and John Skylitzes reports that he stopped soldiers from carrying off local Italians to be sold as slaves in the East.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> he was made patrikios and Domestic of the Schools, in effect commander-in-chief of the army, after the death of Andrew the Scythian. In 895 he led a large army against Tsar Simeon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, though it is unclear whether he fought a battle: a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> invasion stirred up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the navy's operations on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> forced Simeon to seek a truce. Leo VI's Tactica also records a raid he led near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, returning by a different route to avoid the Arab army sent to cut him off.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His death reportedly encouraged Simeon to resume the war, with heavy losses for his successor, Leo Katakalon. Leo VI praised his military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, he was credited with a sharpened stake driven into the ground against cavalry, and Skylitzes called him a brave and prudent man, pious towards God and just towards men. His sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardas-phokas-the-elder">Bardas Phokas the Elder</a> and Leo Phokas the Elder both became Domestics of the Schools, as did his grandsons Nikephoros, later emperor, and Leo Phokas the Younger.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nikephoros Phokas the Elder” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Louis II took Bari from the Arabs in 871, the Byzantines occupied it in 876 and, through the campaigns of <b>Nikephoros Phokas the Elder</b> in 885–886, recovered much of Apulia and Calabria.</blockquote><a href="/state/theme-of-longobardia">Theme of Longobardia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cappadocia. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantion">Byzantium</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=872" aria-label="Byzantium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Nikephoros Phokas the Elder"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=872">What was here in 872 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
